  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule I am reading is: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.” Reply 1 is: ```yaml\\n{\"x\": 1}\\n```\\n{\"y\": 2} . Reply 2 is: {\"y\": 2} . The rule requires Reply 1 to score the fenced object {\"x\": 1}, because a ```yaml fence is a code fence and it holds an object, and that object is the last one inside a fence. The rule requires Reply 2 to score the top-level object {\"y\": 2}, because there is no fence. The check, however, gives the same answer for both: its fence pattern only matches ```json, ```JSON, or a bare ```, so it does not treat the ```yaml fence as a fence. For Reply 1 the fenced pool is empty, and the last top-level object is {\"y\": 2}; for Reply 2 the last top-level object is also {\"y\": 2}. Thus the check returns {\"y\": 2} for both, collapsing a difference the rule requires. I would be wrong if the check returned different answers for these two replies, or if the documented rule were read as excluding ```yaml fences from the phrase “code fence.”",
